Architecture plan for Issue #116: Use the sans font across the entire site

Module structure and file organization
- Tailwind design tokens: update `tailwind.config.ts` so the project's display/body font tokens resolve to the same sans-serif stack, avoiding a split between display and sans typography.
- Global CSS: update `app/globals.css` base/components styles so `body`, headings, eyebrow labels, editorial buttons, and other shared typography helpers inherit/use the sans font rather than display-specific font utilities.
- Public pages and components: inspect and adjust font utility usage in `app/(public)/page.tsx`, `app/(public)/blog/[slug]/page.tsx`, `components/layout/site-header.tsx`, and `components/layout/site-footer.tsx` so no page-level element forces the old display font.
- Tests: add a focused source-level font test under `tests/` that asserts the Tailwind font tokens and key public/layout files no longer use display-font utilities.

Shared types and contracts
- No new runtime data types, API contracts, database schema changes, auth, email, or object-storage behavior are needed.
- The shared contract is visual/design-token consistency: site typography should resolve through the sans font family everywhere.

Dependencies between issues
- Single-issue batch. No dependency on prior feature issues beyond the existing blog UI files being present.

Key design decisions
- Prefer central token/global-style changes over scattered one-off classes where possible, then remove explicit `font-display`/display-font usage from named files.
- Keep all existing layout, content, colors, spacing, and responsive behavior unchanged; only typography family should change.
- Use tests that guard against regressions in the files named by the issue without adding brittle pixel-level/browser assertions.
